{"batchcomplete":true,"query":{"pages":[{"pageid":28377060,"ns":0,"title":"Hinkley groundwater contamination","revisions":[{"revid":1377563909,"parentid":1377563826,"minor":true,"timestamp":"2026-09-30T00:56:29Z","size":43955,"sha1":"69269ed7a33c046cc7ce61b8ba10e8e3306a6458","slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","content":"{{short description|Human-caused environmental event in the US}}\n{{Distinguish|Hinkley Point C nuclear power station}}\n[[File:S2B MSIL1C 20180325 T11SMU Hinkley CA RGB EPSG32610 WGS84.tif|thumb|alt=See caption|Satellite image of Hinkley, Barstow and Harper Lake, California]]\nFrom 1952 to 1966, [[Pacific Gas and Electric Company]] (PG&E) dumped about {{convert|370|e6USgal|L|abbr=off|sp=us}} of [[chromium]]-tainted wastewater into unlined wastewater spreading ponds around the town of [[Hinkley, California]], located in the [[Mojave Desert]] about {{convert|120|mi|km|abbr=off|-1|sp=us}} north-northeast of [[Los Angeles]].<ref name=\"environs_2003\" /><ref name=\"motherjones_2013\">{{Cite web |last=Heath |first=David |date=3 June 2013 |title=Erin Brockovich's Biggest Debunker, Debunked: A closer look finds serious flaws in the research of a scientist trying to disprove an infamous California cancer cluster |url=https://www.motherjones.com/environment/2013/05/erin-brockovich-hinkley-california-junk-science |access-date=13 April 2013 |website=[[Center for Public Integrity]] via Mother Jones}}</ref>\n\nPG&E used chromium 6, or [[hexavalent chromium]] (a cheap and efficient rust suppressor), in its [[compressor station]] for natural-gas transmission pipelines.<ref name=\"environs_2003\">{{Cite journal |date=2003 |title=The 'Erin Brockvich effect': How media shapes toxics policy |url=http://environs.law.ucdavis.edu/volumes/26/2/banks.pdf |journal=Environs |volume=26 |pages=219–32 |number=2}}</ref>{{Rp|}}<ref name=\"digfortruth_2000\">{{Cite web |last=Welkos |first=Robert W. |date=12 March 2000 |title=Digging for the Truth: With tensions over accuracy in film running high, 'Erin Brockovich' pays attention to real-life detail |url=https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-2000-mar-12-ca-7856-story.html |access-date=13 April 2013 |website=[[Los Angeles Times]]}}</ref> Hexavalent-chromium compounds are [[Genotoxicity|genotoxic]] [[carcinogen]]s.\n\nIn 1993, legal clerk [[Erin Brockovich]] began an investigation into the health impacts of the contamination. A class-action lawsuit about the contamination was settled on July 2, 1996 for $333 million (around ${{Format price|{{Inflation|US-GDP|333000000|1993}}}} in {{Inflation/year|US-GDP}}). In 2008, PG&E settled the last of the cases involved with the Hinkley claims. Since then, the town's population has dwindled to the point that in 2016 ''[[The New York Times]]'' described Hinkley as having slowly become a [[ghost town]].<ref name=\"NYT_2016\">{{Cite news |last=Lovett |first=Ian |date=23 January 2016 |title=Gas Leak in Los Angeles Has Residents Looking Warily Toward Flint |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/24/us/gas-leak-in-los-angeles-has-residents-looking-warily-toward-flint.html?_r=0 |access-date=24 January 2016 |work=[[The New York Times]]}}</ref><ref name=\"sbsun_2015\">{{Cite news |last=Steinberg |first=Jim |date=March 18, 2015 |title=Hinkley continues to shrink: Desert town set to lose only market, gas station, Post Office |url=http://www.sbsun.com/environment-and-nature/20150318/hinkley-continues-to-shrink-desert-town-set-to-lose-only-market-gas-station-post-office?source=most_viewed}}</ref>\n\n== History ==\nDuring the early 1950s, Pacific Gas & Electric built its first two compressor stations in [[Topock, Arizona]], and Hinkley at the southern end of what became its trans-California natural-gas transmission system: a network of eight compressor stations linked with {{Convert|40,000|miles|km}} of distribution pipeline and {{Convert|6,000|miles|km}} of transport pipeline. From [[Bakersfield, California|Bakersfield]] to the [[Oregon]] border, the network served 4.2 million customers.<ref name=\"PGE_environment_2015\">{{Cite web |date=2015 |title=Compressor Stations Environmental Restoration Activities at Compressor Station Properties |url=http://www.pge.com/en/about/environment/taking-responsibility/compressor/index.page |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160425085324/http://www.pge.com/en/about/environment/taking-responsibility/compressor/index.page |archive-date=25 April 2016 |access-date=16 April 2016 |publisher=Pacific Gas and Electricity}}</ref> At the Topock and Hinkley compressor stations, a hexavalent chromium additive was used as a rust inhibitor in cooling towers.<ref name=\"PGE_environment_2015\" /> The water was then disposed adjacent to the compressor stations.<ref name=\"PGE_environment_2015\" /> Although the dumping took place from 1952 to 1966 (when Hinkley was a remote desert community with one school and a general store),<ref name=\"environs_2003\" /> PG&E did not inform the local water board about the contamination until December 7, 1987.<ref name=\"publicintegrity_2013\">{{Cite web |last=David Heath |date=March 13, 2013 |title=How industry scientists stalled action on carcinogen |url=https://www.publicintegrity.org/2013/03/13/12290/how-industry-scientists-stalled-action-carcinogen |access-date=14 April 2016 |publisher=Center for Public Integrity}}</ref>\n\nResidents of Hinkley filed a [[class action]] against PG&E, ''Anderson, et al. v. Pacific Gas and Electric'' (Superior Ct. for [[San Bernardino County, California|County of San Bernardino]], Barstow Division, file BCV 00300).<ref name=\"environs_2003\" /> LeRoy A. Simmons was the judge. In 1993, [[Erin Brockovich]] (a legal clerk for lawyer [[Edward L. Masry]]) investigated an apparent cluster of illnesses in the community which were linked to hexavalent chromium.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Baes |first=Michael |date=July 29, 2011 |title=Final Technical Support Document on Public Health Goal for Hexavalent Chromium in Drinking Water |url=http://oehha.ca.gov/water/phg/072911Cr6PHG.html |access-date=April 23, 2012 |website=Water |publisher=California Environmental Protection Agency |quote=The health-protective level is based on avoidance of potential carcinogenic effects |location=[[Oakland, California]]}}</ref> The case was referred to [[arbitration]], with maximum damages of $400 million for more than 600 people. After arbitration for the first 40 people resulted in about $120 million, PG&E reassessed its position and decided to end arbitration and settle the case. It was settled in 1996 for $333 million, the largest settlement of a [[class action]] lawsuit in U.S. history at the time.<ref name=\"sbcsentinel_2013\">{{Cite web |last=Venturi |date=September 28, 2013 |title=PG&E Hit With Class Action Lawsuit Over Lingering Hinkley Contamination |url=http://sbcsentinel.com/2013/09/pge-hit-with-class-action-lawsuit-over-lingering-hinkley-contamination/ |access-date=April 13, 2016 |publisher=San Bernardino County Sentinel |location=Hinkley}}</ref><ref name=\"digfortruth_2000\" /><ref>{{Cite news |date=1996-07-02 |title=PG&E to Pay $333 Million In Pollution Suit |url=https://www.sfgate.com/business/article/PG-E-to-Pay-333-Million-In-Pollution-Suit-3303933.php |access-date=2018-09-18 |work=San Francisco Chronicle}}</ref>\n\n[[File:Detail Hinkley protest sign.jpg|thumb|alt=Hand-painted white billboard in the desert|Protest sign outside Hinkley]]\nDuring negotiations, the presiding judges told PG&E's lawyers that a 1987 study by Chinese scientist Jian Dong Zhang reporting a strong link between chromium 6 pollution and cancer in humans would be \"influential\" in their decision.<ref name=\"zhang_1987\" /><ref name=\"WSJ_2006\" /> In 1997, an article was published in which Zhang retracted his 1987 research.<ref name=\"Zhang_Li_1997\">{{Cite journal |last=Jian Dong Zhang |last2=Shu Kun Li |date=April 1997 |title=Cancer mortality in a Chinese population exposed to hexavalent chromium in water |journal=Journal of Occupational and Environmental Medicine |volume=39 |issue=4 |pages=315–31 |doi=10.1097/00043764-199704000-00008 |pmid=9113601}}{{Retracted|doi=10.1097/00043764-200607000-00019|pmid=16832233|intentional=yes}}</ref> [[ChemRisk]], a firm known to be working with PG&E since 1995, updated his analysis and published it in the April 1997 ''Journal of Occupational and Environmental Medicine'' (JOEM, the official publication of the [[American College of Occupational and Environmental Medicine]]) as a retraction of Zhang's 1987 paper. It was published under Zhang's name (then a retired Chinese government health officer, and despite his written objection) and that of a second Chinese scientist, Shu Kun Li.<ref name=\"Zhang_Li_1997\" /> Peter Waldman, reporter for ''[[The Wall Street Journal]]'', wrote that Zhang's son was \"outraged\" at \"the idea that his father would willingly have invalidated his earlier award-winning work.\"<ref name=\"Egilman_2006\" /><ref name=\"WSJ_2005\" /> According to the [[Center for Public Integrity]], \"In contrast to the earlier article, the new one concluded that chromium wasn't the likely culprit. The revised study—which did not reveal the involvement of PG&E or its scientists—helped persuade California health officials to delay new drinking water standards for chromium.\"<ref name=\"publicintegrity_2013\" />\n\nIn March 2001, the [[California Environmental Protection Agency]] (CalEPA) asked the [[University of California, Berkeley]] to name a panel of experts to form the Chromate Toxicity Review Committee. The [[California Office of Environmental Health Hazard Assessment]] (OEHHA), established in 1991 with the creation of CalEPA, had been located across from UC Berkeley and had maintained academic ties with the school.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Cal/EPA History |url=http://www.calepa.ca.gov/About/History01/oehha.htm |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090520235233/http://www.calepa.ca.gov/About/History01/oehha.htm |archive-date=2009-05-20 |access-date=2009-05-19}}</ref> A meeting was held on July 25, 2001, to obtain \"public input on the review of scientific questions regarding the potential of chromium 6+ to cause cancer when ingested.\"<ref name=\"public_meeting_2001\">{{Cite web |date=July 25, 2001 |title=Public meeting requesting public input on the review of scientific questions regarding the potential of chromium 6+ to cause cancer when ingested |url=http://oehha.ca.gov/public_info/public/chr6meets.html |access-date=April 14, 2016 |website=California Office of Environmental Health Hazard Assessment}}</ref> The panel was selected by [[Jerold A. Last]], with [[Dennis Paustenbach]] as vice-president, and included [[Mark Schrenker]], [[Silvio De Flora]] and [[John Froines]].<ref name=\"bruin1\">{{Cite news |last=Schallert |first=Amanda |date=15 July 2013 |title=UCLA professor resigns from air quality panel |url=http://dailybruin.com/2013/07/15/ucla-professor-resigns-from-air-quality-panel/ |access-date=20 October 2013 |work=University of California, Los Angeles Daily Bruin |location=Los Angeles}}</ref> Paustenbach, De Flora and Froines resigned from the committee and were replaced.<ref name=\"oehha_CTRC_2001\" /><ref name=\"California_senate_2003\">{{Citation |title=Possible Interference in the Scientific Review of Chromium VI Toxicity |date=February 28, 2003 |work=Senate Health and Human Services Committee |series=Senate Hearing |place=Los Angeles, California}}</ref>\n\nOn August 31, 2001, the Chromate Toxicity Review Committee (which then included Russell Flegal, Jerold Last, Ernest E. McConnell of ToxPath,{{Clarify|date=January 2019|reason=What is ToxPath?}} Marc Schenker and Hanspeter Witschi) submitted its report, entitled \"Scientific Review of Toxicological and Human Health Issues Related to the Development of a Public Health Goal for Chromium(VI)\".<ref name=\"oehha_CTRC_2001\">{{Cite web |last=Flegal |first=Russell |last2=Last |first2=Jerold |last3=McConnell ToxPath |first3=Ernest E. |last4=Schenker |first4=Marc |last5=Witschi |first5=Hanspeter |date=August 31, 2001 |title=Scientific Review of Toxicological and Human Health Issues Related to the Development of a Public Health Goal for Chromium(VI) |url=http://oehha.ca.gov/public_info/facts/pdf/crpanelrptfinal901.pdf |access-date=April 14, 2016 |website=Chromate Toxicity Review Committee via OEHHA |page=32}}</ref><ref name=\"Borneff_1968\">{{Cite journal |last=Borneff |first=J |last2=Engelhardt |first2=K |last3=Griem |first3=W |last4=Kunte |first4=H |last5=Reichert |first5=J. |date=1968 |title=Experiment with 3,4-benzopyrene and potassium chromate in mice drink |journal=Arch Hyg Bakteriol |series=Carcinogens in water and soil |volume=152 |pages=45–53 |pmid=5707368 |number=XXII}}</ref> The committee recommended that reports of chromium concentrations (particularly in southern California) were alarmist and \"spuriously high\", and further evaluation should be handled by academics in laboratory settings—not by regulators.<ref name=\"oehha_CTRC_2001\" />{{Rp|29}} The report cited the 1987 Zhang article and its retracted 1997 version.<ref name=\"zhang_1987\" /><ref name=\"WSJ_2006\" /><ref name=\"Zhang_Li_1997\" /> Citing the 2001 Chromate Toxicity Review Committee review<ref name=\"oehha_CTRC_2001\" /> in November of that year, the Office of Environmental Health Hazard Assessment withdrew its 1999 public-health goal for total chromium in drinking water of 2.5 parts per billion.<ref name=\"Egilman_2006\">{{Cite journal |last=Egilman |first=David |date=2006 |title=Corporate Corruption of Science – The Case of Chromium(VI) |url=https://asopossos.files.wordpress.com/2008/11/ijoeh_april06-cr62.pdf |journal=International Journal of Occupational and Environmental Health |series=Commentary |volume=12 |issue=2 |pages=169–76 |doi=10.1179/oeh.2006.12.2.169 |pmid=16722197 |s2cid=27619936}}</ref><ref name=\"California_senate_2003\" />\n\nIn 2001, the law firm of Engstron, Lipscomb and Lack filed a follow-up lawsuit on behalf of 900 people alleging chromium contamination in Hinkley and [[Kettleman City, California|Kettleman]], California.<ref name=\"California_senate_2003\" /> Two years later, Senator [[Deborah Ortiz]] (who represented the Sacramento area and chaired the Senate Health and Human Services Committee) called a Senate hearing about \"Possible Interference in the Scientific Review of Chromium VI Toxicity\".<ref name=\"California_senate_2003\" /> At the hearing, Gary Praglin, a lawyer with Engstron, Lipscomb and Lack in Los Angeles, testified about how the flawed 2001 report by the Chromate Toxicity Review Committee adversely affected the firm's court case against PG&E:<ref name=\"oehha_CTRC_2001\" />\n\n{{Quote | style=font-size:100% |After the blue-ribbon panel report came out, PG&E came into court, and they told the judge that everything had changed. They were wa<!-- i -->ving the blue-ribbon report—the blue-ribbon panel report—like a flag. They said to the judge, the State of California has spoken. It has said that chromium VI does not cause cancer by ingestion, and they wanted to amend their paperwork, their motions, their declarations, and move to dismiss our case. And they got that permission to do that. They amended all their paperwork, and we were given permission to take discovery—to take depositions, issue subpoenas—and we have obtained thousands of pages of documents in connection with the blue-ribbon panel process.}}\n\nSince the Hinkley groundwater contamination lawsuit, a group of California-based scientists with organizations such as the Desert Sierra Cancer Surveillance Program (DSCSP) and ChemRisk have argued against the claim that chromium 6 is genotoxic, downplaying the number of cancer cases and challenging a \"[[cancer cluster]] in the Hinkley area\".<ref name=\"motherjones_2013\" /><ref name=\"cancer\" /><ref name=\"toxic_clout\" /><ref name=\"Patierno_chromium_CPI_2013\" /><ref name=\"NCBI_Patierno_2011\">{{Cite journal |last=Kristen P. Nickens |last2=Steven R. Patierno |last3=Susan Ceryak |date=November 5, 2011 |title=Chromium genotoxicity: a double-edged sword: Cell Death or Survival: The Double-Edged Sword of Environmental Toxicity |journal=Chemico-Biological Interactions |volume=188 |pages=276–88 |doi=10.1016/j.cbi.2010.04.018 |pmc=2942955 |pmid=20430016 |number=2}}</ref>\n\nAccording to the lawsuit, PG&E was also required to discontinue its use of chromium 6 and clean up the contaminated groundwater.<ref name=\"IVEY_engineering_2012\">{{Cite web |date=November 20, 2012 |title=6 Class Action Lawsuits that Changed U.S. History |url=http://www.iveyengineering.com/class-action-lawsuits-2/ |access-date=April 14, 2016 |website=IVEY Engineering}}</ref> By 2008 the chromium plume was spreading, however, and it attracted media attention by 2011.<ref name=\"IVEY_engineering_2012\" /> In November 2010, PG&E began offering to purchase threatened houses and property in Hinkley and to provide [[bottled water]].<ref name=\"IVEY_engineering_2012\" /> By 2013, the plume was over six miles long, two miles wide, and slowly growing.<ref name=\"sbcsentinel_2013\" />\n\nIn 2006, PG&E agreed to pay $295 million to settle cases involving another 1,100 people statewide for hexavalent chromium-related claims. Two years later, it settled the last of the Hinkley claims for $20 million.<ref>{{Cite news |date=April 8, 2008 |title=PG&E settles last chromium(VI) case |work=Los Angeles Times |pages=B2}}</ref> That year, the [[United States Environmental Protection Agency]] (EPA) responded to research by the [[National Toxicology Program]] on the development of cancerous tumors in mice and rats who had consumed heavy doses of chromium 6.<ref name=\"NIEH_2008\">{{Cite report |url=https://ntp.niehs.nih.gov/sites/default/files/ntp/htdocs/lt_rpts/tr546.pdf |title=NTP Technical Report on the Toxicology and Carcinogenesis Studies of Sodium Dichromate Dihydrate in F344/N Rats and B6C3F1 Mice |last=National Toxicology Program |date=July 2008 |page=201 |access-date=April 14, 2016 |number=7789–12–0 |series=Drinking Water Studies |work=National Institutes of Health}}</ref>\n\nCalifornia became the first state to acknowledge a link between ingested hexavalent chromium and cancer in July 2014, establishing a [[maximum contaminant level]] (MCL) for hexavalent chromium of 10 parts per billion (ppb).<ref name=\"HEX Chrome Letter\">{{Cite web |title=State Adoption of a Hexavalent Chromium MCL |url=http://www.waterboards.ca.gov/drinking_water/certlic/drinkingwater/documents/chromium6/LettertoPWS-StateAdoptionofaHexavalentChromiumMCL-20140620.pdf |access-date=2014-10-08 |publisher=California Department of Public Health}}</ref><ref name=\"CalEPA_2015\">{{Cite web |date=2015 |title=Fact Sheet: Frequently Asked Questions about Hexavalent Chromium in Drinking Water |url=http://www.waterboards.ca.gov/drinking_water/certlic/drinkingwater/documents/chromium6/chromium_fact_sheet_2015_final.pdf |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160428041437/http://www.waterboards.ca.gov/drinking_water/certlic/drinkingwater/documents/chromium6/chromium_fact_sheet_2015_final.pdf |archive-date=April 28, 2016 |access-date=April 14, 2016 |website=California Environmental Protection Agency (CalEPA) |location=Sacramento, California}}</ref> Hexavalent chromium is measured in μg/L (micrograms per liter). The EPA does not have an MCL for hexavalent chromium in drinking water, but the agency has an MCL of 100 parts per billion for all forms of [[Chromium#Compounds|chromium]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=2015-09-22 |title=Chromium in Drinking Water |url=https://www.epa.gov/sdwa/chromium-drinking-water |access-date=30 December 2015 |website=[[United States Environmental Protection Agency]]}}</ref>\n\n== Groundwater pollution{{Anchor|Pollution of groundwater}} ==\nPG&E operates a [[compressor station]] in Hinkley for its natural-gas transmission pipelines. The gas must be re-compressed about every {{Convert|350|mi|km}}, and the station uses cooling towers to cool the gas after compression.\n\nBetween 1952 and 1966, the water in the cooling towers contained [[hexavalent chromium]]—now recognized as a carcinogen<ref name=\"CalEPA_2015\" />—to prevent rust in the machinery. The water was stored between uses in unlined ponds, which allowed it to percolate into the [[groundwater]]. This led to [[groundwater pollution]], affecting soil and contaminating water wells near the compressor station with a plume originally about {{Convert|2|mi|km|0|abbr=off|sp=us|spell=on}} long and nearly {{Convert|1|mi|abbr=off|sp=us|spell=in}} wide.<ref name=\"swrcb_2016\">{{Cite web |date=2016 |title=PG&E Hinkley Chromium Cleanup |url=http://www.swrcb.ca.gov/rwqcb6/water_issues/projects/pge/index.shtml |access-date=April 14, 2016 |website=State Water Resources Control Board (SWRCB) via California Environmental Protection Agency}}</ref> By 2013, the plume was {{Convert|6|mi|km|0|abbr=off|sp=us|spell=on}} long and nearly {{Convert|2|mi|km|0|abbr=off|sp=us|spell=on}} wide.<ref name=\"sbcsentinel_2013\" />\n\nAverage hexavalent chromium levels in Hinkley were recorded as 1.19&nbsp;parts-per-billion (ppb), with an estimated peak of 20&nbsp;ppb. Based on the PG&E Background Study, the PG&E Topock Compressor Station averaged 7.8&nbsp;ppb and peaked at 31.8&nbsp;ppb.<ref name=\"SWRCB_2011\">{{Cite web |date=2011 |title=Frequently asked questions about PG&E background chromium study in Hinkley |url=http://www.swrcb.ca.gov/rwqcb6/water_issues/projects/pge/docs/pge_background_study_faq2.pdf |website=California State Water Resources Control Board (SWRCB)}}</ref> The proposed California health goal for hexavalent chromium was 0.02&nbsp;ppb in 2011.<ref name=\"Baes\">{{Cite web |last=Baes |first=Michael  |date=July 29, 2011 |title=Final Technical Support Document on Public Health Goal for Hexavalent Chromium in Drinking Water  |url=http://oehha.ca.gov/water/phg/072911Cr6PHG.html |access-date=April 23, 2012 |website=Water |publisher=California Environmental Protection Agency |quote=The PHG [Public Health Goal] for hexavalent chromium is established at 0.02 parts per billion (ppb) |location=[[Oakland, California]]}}</ref> In 1991, when the EPA raised the federal MCL for total chromium to 100 ppb, California remained at 50 ppb.<ref name=\"SWRCB_2011\" /> According to CalEPA in 2015, \"At the time Total Chromium MCLs were established, ingested Hexavalent Chromium associated with consumption of drinking water was not considered to pose a cancer risk, as is now the case.\"<ref name=\"CalEPA_2015\" />\n\n== Plume ==\nSamples taken in August 2010 showed that the plume of contaminated water had begun to migrate to the lower [[aquifer]].<ref name=\"Brockovich_2010\">{{Cite web |date=November 9, 2010 |title=A decade after \"Erin Brockovich,\" contamination spreads in Hinkley |url=http://www.vvdailypress.com/articles/hinkley-22901-erin-spreads.html |url-status=unfit |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130921055618/http://www.vvdailypress.com/articles/hinkley-22901-erin-spreads.html |archive-date=September 21, 2013 |access-date=November 17, 2010 |website=Victorville Daily Press}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Carrie Kahn |date=December 13, 2010 |title=Erin Brockovich II? Activist Returns To Aid Town |url=https://www.npr.org/2010/12/13/131967600/erin-brockovich-ii-activist-returns-to-aid-town |publisher=[[NPR]]}}</ref> In September 2013, CalEPA reported that the plume had expanded to {{Convert|6|mi}} long and {{Convert|4|mi}} wide.<ref name=\"CalEPA\" /> In 2015, the California Regional Water Quality Control Board, Lahontan Region served PG&E with an order to clean up the effects of the chromium discharge. At the time of the report, the plume was \"{{Convert|8|mi}} in length and approximately {{Convert|2|mi}} in width, throughout the Hinkley Valley and into Harper Dry Lake Valley.\"<ref name=\"CWQCB_2015\">{{Cite web |date=2015 |title=Clean-up and abatement order |url=http://www.waterboards.ca.gov/lahontan/board_decisions/adopted_orders/2015/docs/r6v_2015_0068.pdf |website=The California Regional Water Quality Control Board, Lahontan Region (Water Board) |number=R6V–2015–0068 |location=San Bernardino County}}</ref>{{Rp|2}}\n\n== Cleanup ==\nBy 2013, PG&E had spent over $750 million on remediation. Sheryl Bilbrey, in charge of PG&E remediation efforts, explained why remediation was taking a long time. \"It's a very complex project. We are highly regulated. There are a lot of interested parties. The other thing is it is very important that we get it right.\"<ref name=\"scienceforsale_PBS_2013\" />\n\nFor their 2013 series ''Science for Sale'', ''[[PBS NewsHour]]'' journalist [[Miles O'Brien (journalist)|Miles O'Brien]] interviewed PG&E director of chromium remediation Kevin Sullivan. According to Sullivan, PG&E cleaned up {{Convert|54|acre|ha}}; however, it would take another 40 years before they were done. PG&E built a concrete barrier about a half-mile long to contain the plume, pumped [[ethanol]] into the ground to convert chromium 6 to chromium 3, and planted acres of alfalfa.<ref name=\"scienceforsale_PBS_2013\" /> O'Brien interviewed Erin Brockovich, who was surprised that PG&E had not completed the cleanup it promised over a decade earlier.<ref name=\"scienceforsale_PBS_2013\">{{Cite web |date=March 13, 2013 |title=Protecting Americans From Danger in the Drinking Water |url=https://www.pbs.org/newshour/show/protecting-americans-from-danger-in-the-drinking-water |website=PBS |series=Science for Sale}}</ref> In correspondence with Sullivan, the Water Board noted that by 2014 \"chromium from PG&E's historical releases at the Hinkley Compressor Station has migrated from the upper aquifer to the lower aquifer causing hexavalent chromium concentrations in the lower aquifer to exceed drinking water standards.\"<ref name=\"waterboard_2014\">{{Cite web |date=December 22, 2014 |title=Conditional acceptance of the plan to install a new extraction well to improve the effectiveness of chromium remediation in groundwater in the lower aquifer of Hinkley |url=http://www.waterboards.ca.gov/rwqcb6/water_issues/projects/pge/docs/lower_aquifer_plan_acceptance.pdf |website=California Regional Water Quality Control Board, Lahontan Region (Water Board)}}</ref> Cleanup documentation about Hinkley is maintained on the CalEPA webpage.<ref name=\"CalEPA\">{{Cite web |title=PG&E Hinkley Chromium Cleanup |url=http://www.swrcb.ca.gov/rwqcb6/water_issues/projects/pge/index.shtml |access-date=September 18, 2013 |publisher=[[California Environmental Protection Agency]]}}</ref>\n\n== Debates{{Anchor|Debate on Hinkley cancer clusters and chromium (VI) genotoxicity}} ==\nA 2007 toxicity report for the [[National Toxicology Program]] provided evidence that high doses of chromium 6 caused cancers of the [[gastrointestinal tract]] in rats and mice at levels higher than 5&nbsp;mg/L of water, (5 [[Parts-per notation#Parts-per expressions|ppm]], or 5,000 [[Parts-per notation#Parts-per expressions|ppb]]).<ref name=\"cr6_toxicity\">{{Cite journal |last=Bucher |first=JR |date=January 2007 |title=NTP toxicity studies of sodium dichromate dihydrate (CAS No. 7789-12-0) administered in drinking water to male and female F344/N rats and B6C3F1 mice and male BALB/c and am3-C57BL/6 mice |journal=National Toxicology Program |series=Toxicity Report Series |pages=1–G4 |pmid=17342194 |number=72}}</ref><ref name=\"Zhitkovich_2011\">{{Cite journal |last=Zhitkovich |first=Anatoly |date=2011 |title=Chromium in Drinking Water: Sources, Metabolism, and Cancer Risks |journal=Chemical Research in Toxicology |volume=24 |pages=1617–29 |doi=10.1021/tx200251t |pmc=3196244 |pmid=21766833 |number=10}}</ref>\n\nAverage hexavalent chromium levels in Hinkley were recorded at 1.19&nbsp;ppb, with an estimated peak of 20&nbsp;ppb. The PG&E Topock Compressor Station averaged 7.8&nbsp;ppb, peaking at 31.8&nbsp;ppb (based on the PG&E Background Study).<ref name=\"SWRCB_2011\" /> In 2013, PG&E's Sheryl Bilbery told a PBS journalist: \"There's a lot of scientists that are still debating that question [hexavalent chromium toxicity]. I think that's why the process has taken so long, from what I have read, both at EPA and at the state level. So, I think they're still trying to figure out exactly what is the right answer there.\"<ref name=\"scienceforsale_PBS_2013\" /> In July 2014, CalEPA established a maximum chromium-6 MCL of 10 ppb as a result of new research which linked ingestion of hexavalent chromium with cancer.<ref name=\"CalEPA_2015\" />\n\n=== CalEPA{{Anchor|California Environmental Protection Agency (CalEPA)}} ===\nCalifornia was the first state to put into effect an MCL for hexavalent chromium (chromium 6) in drinking water in July 2014, setting a limit of 10 ppb. A 2015 [[United States Geological Survey]] (USGS) report, based on the EPA's 2010 review of the health effects of chromium 6 in drinking water, re-examined related federal regulations.<ref name=\"USGS_2015\" />{{Rp|9}} Following California's 2014 limit, [[Illinois]] began research in collaboration with the USGS which was published in 2015.<ref name=\"USGS_2015\" />{{Rp|9}} According to the USGS report, the EPA proposed in 2010 to \"classify Cr(VI) as likely to cause cancer in humans when ingested over a lifetime.\"<ref name=\"USGS_2015\" />{{Rp|2}} Concentrations of chromium 6 in water are given in micrograms and milligrams per liter (μg/L and mg/L), with 50&nbsp;μg/L equal to 50 ppb and 50&nbsp;mg/L equal to 50 ppm.<ref name=\"USGS_2015\">{{Cite web |last=Mills |first=Patrick C. |last2=Cobb |first2=Richard P. |date=April 28, 2015 |title=Hexavalent and Total Chromium at Low Reporting Concentrations in Source-Water Aquifers and Surface Waters Used for Public Supply in Illinois, 2013 |url=https://pubs.usgs.gov/sir/2015/5020/pdf/sir2015-5020.pdf |access-date=April 14, 2016 |publisher=United States Geological Survey (USGS) |page=82}}</ref>{{Rp|8}}\n\n=== John Morgan ===\nSince January 1, 1988, all cancers in California have been reported to California Cancer Registry (CCR) regional registries. Hinkley, in San Bernardino County, is covered by the Desert Sierra Cancer Surveillance Program (DSCSP) with the [[California Department of Public Health]].<ref name=\"Morgan_DSCSP_2000\">{{Cite web |last=Morgan |first=John W. |last2=Prendergast |first2=Thomas |date=September 25, 2000 |title=Community Cancer Assessment in Hinkley California, 1988–1993: Hinkley cancer-cluster or not |url=https://assets.documentcloud.org/documents/705830/hinkley-cancer-cluster-or-not.txt |access-date=14 April 2016 |publisher=The Desert Sierra Cancer Surveillance Program (DSCSP)}}</ref> [[Epidemiology|Epidemiologist]] John Morgan began working with the DSCSP in 1995.<ref>{{Citation |title=John Morgan |date=2015 |work=Loma Linda University Health}}</ref>\n\nMorgan has published over 100 abstracts, presentations and reports, some of which have been [[Peer review|peer-reviewed]]. Many of his PowerPoint presentations and online posts attempt to debunk allegations that chromium pollution caused the cancer cluster in the Hinkley area and downplay the number of cancer cases there.<ref name=\"motherjones_2013\" /><ref name=\"cancer\">{{Cite web |last=Morgan |first=John W. |last2=Reeves |first2=M. E. |title=Cancer in Hinkley: What was the real problem? |url=http://cebp.aacrjournals.org/cgi/content/meeting_abstract/20/10_MeetingAbstracts/A78}}</ref> In his often-cited 2010 California Cancer Registry study, Morgan wrote that cancer rates in Hinkley \"remained unremarkable from 1988 to 2008\".<ref name=\"cancer\" /><ref name=\"boston_globe_2010\">{{Cite web |last=Schwartz |first=Naoki |date=December 13, 2010 |title=Survey shows unremarkable cancer rate in CA town |url=http://archive.boston.com/news/nation/articles/2010/12/13/survey_shows_unremarkable_cancer_rate_in_ca_town/ |website=Boston Globe}}</ref> In a study co-authored with M. E. Reeves, he wrote that \"the 196 cases of cancer reported during the most recent survey of 1996 through 2008 were less than what he would expect based on demographics and the regional rate of cancer.\"<ref name=\"cancer\" />\n\nMorgan used cartoon characters in his 2012 PowerPoint presentation at the Proceedings of the North American Association of Central Cancer Registries (NAACCR) Conference, saying: \"Inhaled Cr[VI] powder is accepted as a carcinogen, while the role of aqueous Cr[VI] as a human carcinogen has been challenged.\"<ref name=\"Morgan_2012_conference\">{{Cite conference |last=Morgan |first=John W. |last2=Reeves |first2=M. E. |date=June 2012 |title=Cancer assessments in Hinkley: What's the real problem? |url=http://www.naaccr.org/LinkClick.aspx?fileticket=CUvJNCxwNEM%3d&tabid=271&mid=750 |conference=Proceedings of the North American Association of Central Cancer Registries (NAACCR) Conference |location=Portland, Oregon}}</ref> He wrote a letter to ''The Connection'' to publicize a poster of his.<ref name=\"Morgan_2012\">{{Cite web |date=August 2012 |title=Letter to the Editor. Cancer Assessments in Hinkley, California |url=http://www.ncra-usa.org/files/public/ConnxSummer2012.pdf |website=[[National Cancer Registrars Association]] |series=The Connection}}</ref> In 2013, the [[Center for Public Integrity]] found weaknesses in Morgan's 2010 analysis which challenge the validity of his findings: \"In his first study, he dismisses what others see as a genuine cancer cluster in Hinkley. In his latest analysis, he excludes people who were exposed to the worst contamination.\"<ref name=\"motherjones_2013\" />\n\n=== Dennis Paustenbach ===\n[[Dennis Paustenbach]] has been an expert witness and consultant for a number of companies facing lawsuits over products, environmental practices or product safety.<ref name=\"NYT_2015_unlikely_opponents\" /> Paustenbach was the founder and director of ChemRisk, and Brent Kerger was one of his senior scientists.<ref name=\"Egilman_2006\" /> Their clients included PG&E and [[BP]].<ref name=\"Paustenbach_1996\">{{Cite journal |last=B.D. Kerger |last2=D.J. Paustenbach |last3=G.E. Corbett |last4=B.L. Finley |date=November 1996 |title=Absorption and elimination of trivalent and hexavalent chromium in humans following ingestion of a bolus dose in drinking water |journal=Toxicology and Applied Pharmacology |volume=141 |pages=145–58 |bibcode=1996ToxAP.141..145K |doi=10.1016/S0041-008X(96)80020-2 |pmid=8917687 |number=1}}</ref>\n\nPaustenbach was at the center of a publishing scandal<ref name=\"WSJ_2006\">{{Cite web |last=Waldman |first=Peter |date=June 2, 2006 |title=Publication to Retract An Influential Water Study |url=https://www.wsj.com/articles/SB114921401312569489 |access-date=13 April 2016 |publisher=The Wall Street Journal}}article ID SB113530126572230084</ref> involving the research of Chinese scientist Jian Dong Zhang, who published a paper in 1987 reporting an association between chromium pollution of drinking water and higher rates of [[stomach cancer]] in residents of three villages in [[Liaoning|Liaoning Province]] (in rural northeastern China) who lived near a chromium-ore smelter and drank tainted water for years.<ref name=\"zhang_1987\">{{Cite journal |last=Zhang |first=Jian Dong |last2=Li |first2=XL |date=1987 |title=Chromium pollution of soil and water in Jinzhou |journal=Zhonghua Yu Fang Yi Xue Za Zhi (Chinese Journal of Preventive Medicine) |volume=21 |pages=262–64 |pmid=3443034 |number=5}} Zhonghua Yu Fang Yi Xue Za Zhi translated from Chinese</ref>\n\nAccording to Allan Hirsch, CalEPA's 2008 review of Zhang's 1987 paper agreed with his findings that the rates of stomach cancer in the three villages were significantly higher than those in the province.<ref name=\"scienceforsale_PBS_2013\" /> PG&E hired ChemRisk, a for-profit scientific consulting firm.<ref name=\"NYT_2015_unlikely_opponents\">{{Cite news |date=11 October 2015 |title=Science Consultant Pushes Back Against Unlikely Opponents |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2015/10/12/business/energy-environment/years-after-criticism-of-its-practices-science-consultant-pushes-back.html?smid=pl-share |access-date=13 April 2016 |work=The New York Times}}</ref><ref name=\"huffingtonpost_2013_oct_14\">{{Cite news |last=Foytlin |first=Cherri |date=14 October 2013 |title=ChemRisk, BP and Purple Strategies: A Tangled Web of Not-So-Independent Science |url=http://www.huffingtonpost.com/cherri-foytlin/chemrisk-bp-and-purple-st_b_4095131.html |access-date=13 April 2016 |work=Huffington Post}}</ref>\n\nAccording to the Center for Public Integrity, \"[Zhang's] revised [1997] study—which did not reveal the involvement of PG&E or its scientists—helped persuade California health officials to delay new drinking water standards for chromium.\"<ref name=\"publicintegrity_2013\" /> The EPA cited the article when it allowed the continued use of chromium in a wood preservative, and the [[Agency for Toxic Substances and Disease Registry]] discounted chromium 6 as an oral carcinogen because of the revised study.<ref name=\"Zhang_Li_1997\" />\n\nIn 2006, the ''[[Journal of Occupational and Environmental Medicine]]'' conducted a six-month internal review of the 1997 retraction. Although Zhang had died by the time the ''JOEM'' made its investigation, his co-author agreed that the revised paper should be retracted.<ref name=\"WSJ_2005\" /> The retraction, written by ''JOEM'' editor Paul Brandt-Rauf, said:\n\n<blockquote style=\"font-size:100%\">It has been brought to our attention that an article published in JOEM in the April 1997 issue by Zhang and Li failed to meet the journal's published editorial policy in effect at that time&nbsp;... Specifically, financial and intellectual input to the paper by outside parties was not disclosed.</blockquote>\n\nAccording to a 2005 article by Peter Waldman in ''[[The Wall Street Journal]]'', ChemRisk had authored the article as consultants for PG&E (who were \"being sued for alleged chromium pollution\").<ref name=\"WSJ_2005\">{{Cite web |last=Waldman |first=Peter |date=23 December 2005 |title=Study Tied Pollutant to Cancer; Then Consultants Got Hold of It: 'Clarification' of Chinese Study Absolved Chromium-6; Did Author Really Write It? |url=https://www.wsj.com/articles/SB113530126572230084 |access-date=13 April 2016 |publisher=The Wall Street Journal}}article ID SB113530126572230084</ref> In addition to ChemRisk, Paustenbach later worked with Exponent;<ref name=\"Egilman_2006\" />{{Rp|172}} he and ChemRisk have \"drawn the scrutiny of investigative journalists.\"<ref name=\"NYT_2015_unlikely_opponents\" />\n\n=== Steven Patierno ===\nIn September 2010, EPA scientists concluded that \"even a small amount of a [[chemical compound]] commonly found in [[tap water]] may cause cancer.\"<ref name=\"toxic_clout\">{{Cite web |last=Heath |first=David |last2=Greene |first2=Ronnie |last3=Morris |first3=Jim |last4=Hamby |first4=Chris |title=Toxic clout project |url=https://s3.amazonaws.com/iw-files/documents/pdfs/CPI+Toxic+Clout.pdf |website=[[The Center for Public Integrity]] |editor-first1=Jim |editor-last1=Morris |editor-first2=Ronnie |editor-last2=Greene}}</ref> Steven Patierno, who had been an expert defense witness in seven chromium-6 lawsuits, was named by the EPA to the peer-review panel critiquing the agency's chromium-6 findings in a potential [[conflict of interest]].<ref name=\"Patierno_chromium_CPI_2013\">{{Cite web |last=Heath |first=David |date=February 13, 2013 |title=EPA was unaware of industry ties on cancer review panel |url=https://www.publicintegrity.org/2013/02/13/12184/epa-unaware-industry-ties-cancer-review-panel |access-date=April 14, 2016 |website=Center for Public Integrity}}</ref> A team of investigative journalists from the Center for Public Integrity made the disclosure in a series entitled ''Toxic Clout''.<ref name=\"toxic_clout\" />\n\nPatierno, an expert witness for PG&E, was deputy director of the [[Duke Cancer Institute]] and a former professor of pharmacology at the [[George Washington University School of Medicine & Health Sciences|George Washington University School of Medicine and Health Sciences]] who had conducted a number of studies on chromium and said that \"drinking low doses of chromium (VI) does not cause cancer.\"<ref name=\"toxic_clout\" /><ref name=\"Patierno_chromium_CPI_2013\" /><ref name=\"NCBI_Patierno_2011\" /> Patierno has co-authored papers with ChemRisk's Paustenbach, another expert witness for PG&E. In 1996, Paustenbach and Patierno co-authored an article saying that chromium 6 is not genotoxic.<ref name=\"Paustenbach_Patierno_1996\">{{Cite journal |last=Mirsalis |first=Jon C. |last2=Hamilton |first2=Carol M. |last3=O'Loughlin |first3=Kathleen G. |last4=Paustenbach |first4=Dennis J. |last5=Kerger |first5=Brent D. |last6=Patierno |first6=Steven |date=21 December 1996 |title=Chromium (VI) at plausible drinking water concentrations is not genotoxic in the in vivo bone marrow micronucleus or liver unscheduled DNA synthesis assays |journal=Environmental and Molecular Mutagenesis |volume=28 |pages=60–63 |bibcode=1996EnvMM..28...60M |doi=10.1002/(SICI)1098-2280(1996)28:1<60::AID-EM9>3.0.CO;2-I |pmid=8698048 |s2cid=42029257 |number=1}}<!-- |access-date=13 April 2016 --></ref>\n\n== Film{{Anchor|In media}} ==\nThe contamination and lawsuit became the subject of ''[[Erin Brockovich (film)|Erin Brockovich]]'', a 2000 biographical film which starred [[Julia Roberts]] as Brockovich.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Erin Brockovich and Hexavalent Chromium |url=https://www.skillmd.com/erin-brockovich-and-hexavalent-chromium/ |access-date=2019-05-26 |website=SkillMD |language=en-US |archive-date=2020-08-18 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200818071137/https://www.skillmd.com/erin-brockovich-and-hexavalent-chromium/ |url-status=dead }}</ref> The film was a critical success, with Roberts winning a [[Golden Globe Award for Best Actress in a Motion Picture – Drama]]<ref name=\"lyman3\">{{Cite news |last=Lyman |first=Rick |date=January 22, 2001 |title=Surprises but No Dominator at the Golden Globes |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2001/01/22/movies/surprises-but-no-dominator-at-the-golden-globes.html?scp=55&sq=%22Erin+Brockovich%22&st=nyt |access-date=March 4, 2010 |work=[[The New York Times]]}}</ref> and an [[Academy Award for Best Actress]].<ref name=\"lyman5\">{{Cite news |last=Lyman |first=Rick |date=March 26, 2001 |title=Oscar Spreads the Wealth, but ''Gladiator'' Takes Top Prize; Julia Roberts Is Named Best Actress, And Russell Crowe Is Chosen Best Actor |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2001/03/26/movies/oscar-spreads-wealth-but-gladiator-takes-top-prize-julia-roberts-named-best.html?scp=75&sq=%22Erin+Brockovich%22&st=nyt |access-date=March 4, 2010 |work=[[The New York Times]]}}</ref><ref name=\"lyman4\">{{Cite news |last=Lyman |first=Rick |date=February 14, 2001 |title=''Gladiator'', ''Crouching Tiger'' and Soderbergh Are Oscar Nominees |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2001/02/14/movies/gladiator-crouching-tiger-and-soderbergh-are-oscar-nominees.html?scp=60&sq= |access-date=March 4, 2010 |work=[[The New York Times]]}}</ref> Brockovich, who said that the film was about 98 percent accurate, made a cameo appearance.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Tunzelmann |first=Alex von |date=2013-02-28 |title=Erin Brockovich: jury out on the details |url=https://www.theguardian.com/film/filmblog/2013/feb/28/erin-brokovich-jury-out-details |access-date=2018-09-27 |website=the Guardian |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=2008-07-21 |title=Erin Brockovich&nbsp;– Did The Movie Really Get It Right? |url=https://www.cnbc.com/2008/07/21/erin-brockovich-did-the-movie-really-get-it-right.html |access-date=2018-09-27 |work=CNBC}}</ref>\n\n== See also ==\n* [[Environmental issues in the United States]]\n* [[Toxic hotspot]]\n* [[Flint water crisis]]\n* [[Water pollution]]\n* [[Merchants of Doubt]]\n*[[Love Canal]]\n\n== References ==\n{{Reflist}}\n\n== External links ==\n* [https://hinkleygroundwater.com/ Hinkley Groundwater Remediation Program]\n{{Pacific Gas and Electric Company}}\n{{Coord|34.903|-117.160|display=title}}\n\n{{DEFAULTSORT:Hinkley Groundwater Contamination}}\n[[Category:1996 in California]]\n[[Category:Arbitration cases]]\n[[Category:1996 in the environment]]\n[[Category:Chromium]]\n[[Category:Environment of California]]\n[[Category:Environmental disasters in the United States]]\n[[Category:History of the Mojave Desert region]]\n[[Category:Mojave Desert]]\n[[Category:Pacific Gas and Electric Company]]\n[[Category:San Bernardino County, California]]\n[[Category:Water pollution in the United States]]"}}}],"contentmodel":"wikitext","pagelanguage":"en","pagelanguagehtmlcode":"en","pagelanguagedir":"ltr","touched":"2026-11-17T19:45:02Z","lastrevid":1377563909,"length":43955}]}}